# Untitled

PROTEIN DESIGN
## Dreaming ideal protein structures
A deep learning algorithm for protein structure prediction is used in reverse for de novo protein design.
Noelia Ferruz and Birte Höcker
Proteins are the universal building blocks of life. They act in essentially every cellular process and have manifold uses in biotechnology. So far, most protein engineering efforts have focused on altering an existing protein or enzyme to confer a desired function1. Despite impressive advances in recent years2, de novo protein design — building proteins from scratch — has been successful in only a few dozen cases. In all of these cases, engineering proceeded by designing the protein backbone, sequence and, when possible, function in consecutive steps. A recent paper by Anishchenko et al. in Nature3 shifts away from this paradigm by allowing the simultaneous design of sequence and structure using deep learning. This work is a major step toward building de novo proteins in a high-throughput fashion.
Current de novo protein designs have been generated by mathematically solving an optimization problem: searching for the global minimum defined by an energy function that captures the interactions of atoms in the protein. However, this optimization process is usually solved by employing approximate energy functions and heuristic search algorithms owing to the intractable nature of the calculations. Now, deep learning methods increasingly provide a means to embark on this journey from a fresh angle because they entirely rely on being trained on thousands — and increasingly often millions — of input examples, from which they adapt and learn without following explicit instructions. Although these methods often pose a challenge for the interpretability of the underlying learned models and have been termed ‘black boxes’, they are enabling unprecedented advances in all fields, and protein research is no exception. The recent development of trRosetta4, RoseTTAFold5 and AlphaFold6 has enabled accurate prediction of protein structures. The results of these studies have generated excitement among researchers and beyond, prompting breathless coverage across the media and even being heralded as a “solution to a 50-year-old grand challenge in biology,
which will empower a new generation of molecular biologists77.
The protein design field is also benefiting from exciting advances in artificial intelligence, with several proteins being designed using deep neural networks8. The foundational work from Anishchenko et al. offers a new approach to protein engineering that is likely to spur many new lines of investigation: the design of de novo proteins by entirely shedding physicochemical energy functions and exploiting learned potentials from characterized experimental data. The approach has been inspired by DeepDream9, a convolutional neural network from Google capable of generating mesmerizing, psychedelic — and sometimes even disturbing — images that encompass patterns of animals or faces composing otherwise standard pictures, such as the Mona Lisa. DeepDream had been previously trained on thousands of photos containing faces and other patterns and can ‘dream’ by tuning an arbitrary input image in a step-wise fashion until it produces a face. The result is that the network does not deliver a face per se, but the combination of other patterns that together produce what
the network considers an ‘ideal’ face. These images are also referred to as hallucinations. Inspired by this concept, Anishchenko et al. apply a similar procedure to explore whether trRosetta can ‘dream’ or ‘hallucinate’ ideal protein structures. Given that trRosetta has been trained on protein structures from the Protein Data Bank repository, it plausibly has learned the concept of ideal protein structures. To test their hypothesis, the authors created a dataset of 2,000 random protein sequences, all 100 amino acids in length. They then fed them to trRosetta, whose output predicts the distribution of distances and orientation between all pairs of residues, which can be represented in two-dimensional contact maps (Fig. 1). Because the input sequences are random, the initial contact maps (step 0) show no patterns. In an iterative process, the sequences are mutated one random residue at a time. The mutation is accepted whenever the contrast between the trRosetta predicted distribution of inter-residue distances and a generic distribution averaged over all proteins meets a specific requirement — in this case, whether it passes the Monte Carlo
Metropolis criterion. More simply put, accepted mutations tend to produce sharper contact maps than the previous step (Fig. 1, differences between step 0 and 40,000). From the generated 2,000 output structures, the authors selected 129 that they expressed in Escherichia coli, purified, and analyzed; 27 of these yielded proteins with a circular dichroism spectra consistent with the predicted structures and size-exclusion chromatography peaks corresponding to monomeric or small oligomeric species. Three-dimensional structures were further determined for three of the proteins, two by X-ray crystallography and one by nuclear magnetic resonance spectroscopy, and they closely matched the ‘hallucinated’ models.
The use of trRosetta, a convolutional neural network trained to predict accurate structures from input sequences, is a pioneering approach for designing de novo proteins. The method has the potential to expand the frontiers of de novo design by substantially deviating from the traditional physicochemical approach. Being able to design specific proteins in a fast and cost-effective manner would transform protein engineering and its application to tackle many of the challenges society faces
today1. The prospects are inspiring and raise several questions. Will it be possible to gain control over the direction of the generative process? Will we be able to generate protein architectures not seen during the training process? And finally, and perhaps most importantly, can this be expanded to design de novo sequence structure and function, such as catalytic activities, in a simultaneous process?
The next steps in protein design to achieve these goals are not without their challenges. Current deep learning generative models suffer from a lack of control, and hence the direction the structure design process will take is unpredictable. Besides, de novo designs usually display idealized structures lacking structural embodiments such as flexible loops, which makes it challenging to introduce functional sites and binding interfaces. Lastly, we are still far from developing methodologies that enable designing proteins that perform a specific function — particularly chemical reactions not found in nature — and build the structures and sequences for it.
Nonetheless, the work by Anishchenko et al. and others suggests that artificial intelligence is beginning to truly transform
protein engineering. It augurs an era when protein engineers will be able to dream up de novo proteins with novel structures and functions at an unprecedented pace. ❐
